E.append(topic("topic-neuromuscular-patterns", "Neuromuscular patterns: nerve, root, plexus, junction and muscle", "disease-pattern",
 "Weakness with a normal brain and cord comes from one of five levels, the anterior horn cell, the root or plexus, the peripheral nerve, the neuromuscular junction and the muscle, and each level has a signature in the distribution of weakness, the presence or absence of sensory loss, the reflexes, the pattern of wasting and fasciculation, the fatigability and the electrophysiology. Motor neuron disease combines lower motor neuron signs with upper motor neuron signs and spares sensation; neuropathy is length-dependent and sensory; radiculopathy follows a root and its reflex; junction disease fluctuates and spares reflexes and sensation; and myopathy is proximal, symmetric and painless with normal sensation. Nerve conduction studies and electromyography then confirm the level and separate axonal from demyelinating disease.",
 [("Anterior horn cell and root", "Motor neuron disease produces asymmetric wasting, fasciculation and weakness beginning in one limb or the bulbar muscles, with brisk reflexes in wasted muscles and extensor plantars from the accompanying upper motor neuron degeneration, and no sensory loss; a split hand with wasting of the thenar side, a brisk jaw jerk and a wasted fasciculating tongue are characteristic, and EMG shows widespread denervation and reinnervation in three regions. Radiculopathy produces pain radiating along a dermatome, weakness in a myotome that crosses several nerves (C7 weakens triceps, wrist flexors and finger extensors), sensory loss in the dermatome and loss of the corresponding reflex (C5-6 biceps and brachioradialis, C7 triceps, L3-4 knee, S1 ankle); the sensory nerve action potential is normal because the lesion is proximal to the dorsal root ganglion, the finding that separates a root from a plexus lesion."),
  ("Plexus and peripheral nerve", "Plexopathy produces weakness and sensory loss in the territory of several nerves and several roots at once (the upper trunk: C5-6 muscles of the shoulder and elbow flexion; the lower trunk: intrinsic hand muscles and the medial forearm), with reduced sensory potentials because the lesion is distal to the ganglion; trauma, tumor infiltration, radiation and the inflammatory neuralgic amyotrophy are the causes. Mononeuropathies follow one nerve's territory and are usually entrapments at known sites (median at the carpal tunnel, ulnar at the elbow, radial at the spiral groove, peroneal at the fibular neck), and mononeuritis multiplex from vasculitis picks off nerves one by one. Polyneuropathy is length-dependent and symmetric, beginning in the feet with sensory loss, absent ankle jerks and later weakness; axonal neuropathies (diabetes, alcohol, drugs) reduce amplitudes, whereas demyelinating neuropathies (Guillain-Barre, CIDP, hereditary) slow conduction and block it, and a rapidly ascending paralysis with areflexia is Guillain-Barre until proved otherwise."),
  ("Neuromuscular junction and muscle", "Myasthenia gravis produces fatigable weakness of the eyelids, extraocular muscles, face, bulbar muscles and proximal limbs that worsens through the day and with exercise and improves with rest, with normal reflexes and sensation; acetylcholine receptor or MuSK antibodies, decrement on repetitive stimulation and jitter on single-fiber EMG confirm it, and a thymoma must be sought. Lambert-Eaton syndrome is the presynaptic mirror image: proximal weakness that improves briefly with exercise, absent reflexes that return after contraction, dry mouth and an underlying small cell lung cancer, with increment on high-frequency stimulation. Botulism descends from the cranial nerves with dilated pupils. Myopathies produce symmetric proximal weakness (difficulty rising from a chair, climbing stairs, lifting the arms) with preserved reflexes until late, normal sensation, sometimes myalgia, a raised creatine kinase and small polyphasic motor units on EMG; inflammatory, toxic (statins), endocrine, dystrophic and metabolic causes are separated by distribution, rash, family history and biopsy."),
  ("The examination and the electrophysiology", "The bedside sequence is: is the weakness upper or lower motor neuron; if lower, is there sensory loss (nerve, root, plexus) or not (anterior horn, junction, muscle); is the distribution distal and symmetric (polyneuropathy), proximal and symmetric (myopathy or junction), in one nerve or root, or multifocal; are reflexes lost early (neuropathy, Lambert-Eaton, Guillain-Barre) or preserved (myopathy, myasthenia, motor neuron disease early); and does it fatigue or fluctuate (junction). Nerve conduction studies separate axonal loss (small amplitudes) from demyelination (slow velocities, prolonged distal latencies and F waves, conduction block) and localize entrapments; EMG separates neurogenic (large, reduced-recruitment units with fibrillations) from myopathic (small, early-recruiting units) patterns and finds subclinical denervation in motor neuron disease. Creatine kinase, antibody panels, genetic testing, nerve or muscle biopsy and imaging of the root or plexus complete the localization.")],
 ["Sensory loss excludes anterior horn cell, junction and muscle; its absence with lower motor neuron signs points to them", "A normal sensory nerve action potential in a numb dermatome means the lesion is at the root, proximal to the ganglion", "Fatigable ptosis and diplopia with normal reflexes is myasthenia; proximal weakness that improves with exercise and returns reflexes is Lambert-Eaton", "Length-dependent symmetric sensory loss with absent ankle jerks is polyneuropathy; asymmetric wasting with brisk reflexes is motor neuron disease", "Proximal, symmetric, painless weakness with normal sensation and a raised creatine kinase is muscle"],
 ["Look at the tongue, the hands and the gait before touching the reflex hammer: fasciculation, split hand and foot drop tell the level", "Ascending weakness with lost reflexes is Guillain-Barre: measure the vital capacity, not just the strength", "Do not stop a statin without a creatine kinase, and do not blame the statin without one"],
 [B(15, 129, 140), B(16, 141, 156), B(17, 157, 166), B(27, 281, 288), B(28, 289, 292), B(29, 293, 298), B(30, 299, 308), S(3, 71, 130), S(4, 165, 167)], meshIds=["brachial-plexus-l", "brachial-plexus-r", "median-nerve-l", "median-nerve-r", "ulnar-nerve-l", "ulnar-nerve-r", "sciatic-nerve-l", "sciatic-nerve-r", "spinal-grey-anterior-horn"],
 imaging=("Imaging is reserved for the root and plexus: MRI of the spine for radiculopathy, MR neurography or ultrasound of the plexus and entrapment sites, chest CT for thymoma and lung cancer, and muscle MRI for the pattern of edema and fatty replacement in myopathy; the main tests are electrophysiological.",
  [pathol("Cervical or lumbar radiculopathy", "MRI", "Disc protrusion or foraminal stenosis compressing the root at the level predicted by the dermatome and reflex; asymptomatic degenerative change is common, so the clinical level rules.", sequence="T2"),
   pathol("Brachial plexopathy", "MRI", "Thickening and T2 hyperintensity of the trunks or cords with enhancement in inflammatory or infiltrative disease; ultrasound shows hourglass constrictions in neuralgic amyotrophy.", sequence="STIR"),
   pathol("Inflammatory myopathy", "MRI", "Symmetric muscle edema on STIR in the thighs with fatty replacement in chronic disease; guides the biopsy site.", sequence="STIR"),
   pathol("Thymoma in myasthenia gravis", "CT", "Anterior mediastinal mass on chest CT in a patient with acetylcholine receptor antibodies.", sequence="CT contrast")]),
 structures=["spinal-grey-anterior-horn", "dorsal-root-ganglion", "plexus-brachial", "plexus-lumbosacral", "neuromuscular-junction", "motor-unit", "nerve-median", "nerve-ulnar", "nerve-common-peroneal"], syndromes=["syn-als-pattern", "syn-guillain-barre", "syn-myasthenia-gravis", "syn-lambert-eaton", "syn-myopathy-pattern", "syn-length-dependent-polyneuropathy", "syn-radiculopathy-cervical", "syn-radiculopathy-lumbosacral", "syn-carpal-tunnel", "syn-mononeuritis-multiplex"], topics=["topic-nerve-injury-regeneration", "topic-neurotransmitter-systems"],
 pitfalls=["Myasthenia has normal reflexes and no wasting; a wasted hand with brisk reflexes is motor neuron disease, however fatigable the patient feels"], tags=["neuromuscular", "EMG", "localization"]))

E.append(topic("topic-pediatric-neurological-syndromes", "Pediatric neurological syndromes", "pediatric",
 "The child's nervous system differs from the adult's in its unfinished myelination, its open sutures, its immature reflexes and its dependence on developmental milestones as the measure of function, so the same anatomy produces different clinical pictures: a hemispheric lesion produces hemiplegic cerebral palsy rather than a stroke syndrome, raised pressure produces a bulging fontanelle and a big head rather than papilledema, and a metabolic disease produces regression of milestones rather than dementia. The commonest pediatric syndromes, cerebral palsy, hydrocephalus and Chiari, febrile and epileptic seizures, neurocutaneous disorders, posterior fossa tumors, floppy infant syndromes and the leukodystrophies, are each anchored to atlas structures and a developmental timetable.",
 [("The developing examination", "Primitive reflexes (Moro, grasp, asymmetric tonic neck, stepping) are present at birth and disappear by six months as the corticospinal tract myelinates; their persistence or asymmetry is the earliest sign of an upper motor neuron lesion, and the Babinski sign is normal until walking begins. Tone evolves from the flexor hypertonia of the newborn to the hypotonia of early infancy and then to adult tone; a floppy infant with weakness has a lower motor neuron disorder (spinal muscular atrophy with tongue fasciculation and absent reflexes, congenital myopathy, myasthenia, botulism), whereas a floppy infant with preserved strength and brisk reflexes has a central cause (hypoxic injury, chromosomal disorder, metabolic disease). Milestones (social smile at 6 weeks, sitting at 6 to 8 months, walking at 12 to 18 months, words at 12 months, sentences at 2 years) are the functional examination, and their loss, regression, is the hallmark of a degenerative or metabolic disease and must never be attributed to behavior."),
  ("Cerebral palsy, hydrocephalus and the posterior fossa", "Cerebral palsy is a non-progressive motor disorder from injury to the developing brain: spastic diplegia from periventricular leukomalacia of prematurity (the leg fibers of the corticospinal tract lie closest to the ventricle), spastic hemiplegia from perinatal middle cerebral artery stroke, spastic quadriplegia from diffuse hypoxic-ischemic injury, dyskinetic palsy from basal ganglia and thalamic injury in term asphyxia or kernicterus, and ataxic palsy from cerebellar malformation. Infant hydrocephalus presents with an enlarging head, a tense fontanelle, splayed sutures, sunsetting eyes from posterior commissure compression and irritability; aqueductal stenosis, Chiari II with myelomeningocele, post-hemorrhagic hydrocephalus of prematurity and posterior fossa tumors are the causes. Posterior fossa tumors (medulloblastoma of the vermis, pilocytic astrocytoma of the hemisphere, ependymoma of the fourth ventricle, diffuse pontine glioma) present with morning vomiting, ataxia, head tilt and cranial nerve palsies."),
  ("Seizures and neurocutaneous syndromes", "Febrile seizures are brief generalized seizures with fever between six months and five years in a neurologically normal child and carry a small risk of later epilepsy; complex febrile seizures (focal, prolonged, repeated) and a first afebrile seizure need more evaluation. Infantile spasms (flexor jerks in clusters with hypsarrhythmia on EEG) signal a serious cause such as tuberous sclerosis or a malformation and regress development unless treated; childhood absence epilepsy, benign epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes and juvenile myoclonic epilepsy are the common age-related syndromes with characteristic EEGs. The neurocutaneous disorders link skin to brain: neurofibromatosis type 1 (cafe-au-lait spots, optic pathway gliomas, learning difficulty), tuberous sclerosis (hypomelanotic macules, cortical tubers, subependymal nodules and giant cell astrocytomas, infantile spasms), Sturge-Weber (facial port-wine stain with leptomeningeal angioma, seizures and hemiparesis) and neurofibromatosis type 2 (bilateral vestibular schwannomas)."),
  ("Metabolic, degenerative and acquired disorders", "Inborn errors present with encephalopathy, seizures and regression, often precipitated by fasting or illness: urea cycle and organic acid disorders in the newborn, mitochondrial disorders with stroke-like episodes and lactic acidosis (MELAS), lysosomal storage diseases with hepatosplenomegaly and cherry-red spots, and the leukodystrophies (metachromatic, Krabbe, adrenoleukodystrophy) with progressive spasticity and white-matter change on MRI. Acquired disorders include bacterial meningitis (group B streptococcus and E. coli in neonates, pneumococcus and meningococcus later), herpes encephalitis, acute disseminated encephalomyelitis after infection, Guillain-Barre syndrome, and non-accidental injury, which must be considered in any infant with subdural hemorrhage, retinal hemorrhages and fractures. Neonatal hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy affects the watershed cortex in mild and the deep gray matter in severe insults, and its grading determines cooling and prognosis.")],
 ["Regression of milestones is never behavioral: it is metabolic, degenerative or epileptic until proved otherwise", "A floppy infant who is weak has a lower motor neuron disease; a floppy infant who is strong has a brain disease", "Spastic diplegia is the signature of prematurity because the leg fibers run beside the ventricles", "Sunset eyes in an infant are Parinaud syndrome from hydrocephalus stretching the posterior commissure", "Infantile spasms need an EEG and a skin examination under Wood's lamp today, not next month"],
 ["Measure the head circumference at every visit; the curve is the child's intracranial pressure record", "Morning vomiting with a head tilt in a child is a posterior fossa tumor until the MRI is done", "Retinal hemorrhages plus subdural blood in an infant means abuse until a rigorous alternative is proven"],
 [S(18, 486, 506), S(4, 160, 167), S(16, 442, 452), B(25, 269, 274), B(18, 167, 172), B(24, 257, 262), B(31, 309, 312), B(20, 207, 216)], meshIds=["ventricle-lateral-l", "ventricle-lateral-r", "cerebellar-vermis-vi", "cerebellar-lobule-x-l", "cerebellar-lobule-x-r", "brainstem", "posterior-commissure", "spinal-grey-anterior-horn"],
 imaging=("Cranial ultrasound through the fontanelle for hemorrhage and ventricular size in infants, MRI for malformations, injury patterns and white-matter disease staged against the myelination timetable, and CT only for acute trauma or shunt failure.",
  [pathol("Periventricular leukomalacia", "MRI", "Loss of periventricular white matter with irregular ventricular margins and thinning of the posterior corpus callosum in an ex-premature child with spastic diplegia.", sequence="T2"),
   pathol("Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (term)", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion and T1 hyperintensity of the posterior putamen, ventrolateral thalamus and perirolandic cortex in severe insults; watershed cortex in partial prolonged insults.", sequence="DWI", timing="DWI peaks at 3-5 days; T1 changes appear after a week"),
   pathol("Medulloblastoma", "MRI", "Enhancing midline vermian mass filling the fourth ventricle with restricted diffusion and hydrocephalus; spine imaging for drop metastases.", sequence="T1+Gd"),
   pathol("Tuberous sclerosis", "MRI", "Cortical tubers (FLAIR-bright expanded gyri), subependymal nodules along the ventricles and a subependymal giant cell astrocytoma at the foramen of Monro.", sequence="FLAIR"),
   pathol("Non-accidental injury", "CT", "Subdural hematomas of different ages, interhemispheric blood and hypoxic injury with retinal hemorrhages and skeletal fractures.", sequence="CT non-contrast")]),
 structures=["ventricle-lateral", "cerebral-aqueduct", "posterior-commissure", "cerebellar-vermis", "cerebellar-lobule-x", "brainstem", "corona-radiata", "spinal-grey-anterior-horn"], syndromes=["syn-hydrocephalus", "syn-chiari-tonsillar-ectopia", "syn-parinaud-dorsal-midbrain", "syn-cerebellar-vermian-midline", "syn-guillain-barre"], topics=["topic-neural-development-malformations", "topic-leukodystrophies-mitochondrial", "topic-epilepsy-localization", "topic-cns-infections-localization"],
 pitfalls=["Unmyelinated white matter in an infant is bright on T2 and must not be called edema or leukodystrophy; compare with the myelination expected for age"], tags=["pediatric", "development", "cerebral palsy"]))

E.append(topic("topic-localization-approach-exam", "Localization: the neurological examination as a map", "approach",
 "Neurological diagnosis answers two questions in order, where is the lesion and what is it, and the first is answered by the examination read against the anatomy: the level (cortex, subcortical white matter, basal ganglia, thalamus, brainstem, cerebellum, spinal cord, root, plexus, nerve, junction, muscle), the side (crossed or uncrossed pathways), and the number of lesions (one, multiple or diffuse). Each examination component tests a system whose anatomy this atlas shows, and combinations of findings that can only be explained by one site are the strongest evidence; the pattern of onset (sudden, stepwise, progressive, relapsing) then narrows the pathology. This entry summarizes the logic that the syndrome entries apply case by case.",
 [("Level: which floor of the building", "Cortical lesions add cortical signs (aphasia, neglect, apraxia, agnosia, field defects, seizures, cortical sensory loss) and produce weakness that favors one body part (face and arm or leg); subcortical capsular lesions produce equal face-arm-leg weakness without cortical signs; basal ganglia lesions produce movement disorders rather than weakness; thalamic lesions produce hemisensory loss of all modalities including the face; brainstem lesions produce crossed signs (a cranial nerve on one side with long-tract signs on the other), vertigo, diplopia and dysarthria; cerebellar lesions produce ipsilateral ataxia without weakness; cord lesions produce a sensory level, bilateral signs below the lesion, sphincter change and dissociated sensory loss; and peripheral lesions produce lower motor neuron signs in the distribution of a root, plexus, nerve or a length-dependent pattern with sensory loss. The upper versus lower motor neuron distinction (tone, reflexes, plantar responses, wasting, fasciculation) is the first branch of the tree."),
  ("Side: the crossings", "The corticospinal tract crosses at the pyramidal decussation, the dorsal column pathway in the lower medulla, the spinothalamic tract within the cord one or two segments above entry, the trigeminal pathways in the pons and medulla, the optic pathway partially at the chiasm, the cerebellar outflow in the superior cerebellar peduncle (so cerebellar signs are ipsilateral), and the cranial nerve nuclei project to the same side; therefore weakness contralateral to a facial palsy that includes the forehead means a pontine lesion, pain loss on one side of the face and the other side of the body means a lateral medullary lesion, and weakness with proprioceptive loss on one side and pain loss on the other means a hemicord lesion. Gaze deviation toward the weak side means a pontine lesion, away from it a hemispheric one. Sensory levels for pin lie one or two segments below the lesion, and a Horner syndrome is ipsilateral to any lesion of the sympathetic pathway."),
  ("Number and time course", "A single lesion is sought first; when findings cannot be explained by one site (optic neuritis plus a cord level, or a facial palsy plus a foot drop), the process is multifocal (multiple sclerosis, vasculitis, metastases, emboli) or diffuse (encephalopathy, polyneuropathy, myopathy). The tempo names the pathology: seconds to minutes and maximal at onset is vascular (ischemia, hemorrhage) or a seizure; minutes to hours with spread is migraine or seizure; hours to days is inflammatory, infectious or toxic-metabolic; weeks to months is neoplastic or subacute degenerative; months to years is degenerative or genetic; and relapsing-remitting is demyelinating, vascular or paroxysmal. Age, risk factors and the systemic examination (fever, rash, lymphadenopathy, murmurs, skin stigmata) complete the differential before any test."),
  ("The examination as a system check", "Mental status tests the cortex and its networks (attention, language, memory, visuospatial, executive); the cranial nerves test the brainstem level by level (II and III midbrain, V-VIII pons, IX-XII medulla) and the special senses; the motor examination tests tone, power by segment, reflexes and the plantar response; the sensory examination tests the two ascending systems separately (pin and temperature for the spinothalamic, vibration and position for the dorsal columns) and the cortical modalities; coordination tests the cerebellum and its inputs; and gait integrates everything (hemiparetic, spastic, ataxic, parkinsonian, steppage, waddling, apraxic and functional gaits each have a site). A focused examination driven by the history takes ten minutes, and the atlas's syndrome entries show how each pattern is assembled from these findings.")],
 ["Where before what: level, side and number, then tempo", "Cortical signs place the lesion in the cortex; equal face-arm-leg weakness without them places it in the capsule", "Crossed findings mean brainstem; a sensory level means cord; ipsilateral ataxia means cerebellum", "Sudden and maximal at onset is vascular; progressive over weeks is a mass; relapsing is demyelination", "If one lesion cannot explain everything, look for a multifocal or diffuse process"],
 ["Examine the gait first: it screens the whole nervous system in ten steps", "The forehead decides the facial palsy, the pupil decides the third nerve, the plantar decides the motor neuron", "Never let imaging replace the examination in the brainstem, where the first scan is often normal"],
 [B(1, 1, 10), B(3, 25, 32), B(4, 33, 40), S(4, 143, 167), S(5, 218, 224), S(11, 356, 362)], meshIds=["gyrus-precentral-l", "gyrus-precentral-r", "internal-capsule-l", "internal-capsule-r", "brainstem", "spinal-cord", "cerebellar-hemisphere-l", "cerebellar-hemisphere-r"],
 imaging=("Imaging is chosen by the localization: MRI with DWI for brain and brainstem, MRI of the appropriate spinal level for cord and root, CT for acute hemorrhage and bone, vascular imaging when the tempo is vascular, and electrophysiology for the peripheral levels.",
  [pathol("Capsular lacune versus cortical infarct", "MRI", "A small deep restricted-diffusion lesion in the posterior limb explains face-arm-leg weakness without cortical signs; a cortical lesion explains aphasia or neglect with partial weakness.", sequence="DWI"),
   pathol("Brainstem lesion with crossed signs", "MRI", "Paramedian or lateral brainstem lesion at the level named by the cranial nerve; DWI may lag the examination by a day.", sequence="DWI"),
   pathol("Spinal cord lesion with a sensory level", "MRI", "Intramedullary signal change or extrinsic compression at or above the clinical level, imaged with sagittal T2 and STIR of the whole cord when the level is uncertain.", sequence="T2")]),
 structures=["gyrus-precentral", "internal-capsule", "thalamus", "brainstem", "cerebellum", "spinal-cord", "pyramid", "medial-lemniscus"], pathways=["pathway-lateral-corticospinal", "pathway-dcml", "pathway-lateral-spinothalamic", "pathway-corticobulbar", "pathway-trigeminothalamic"], syndromes=["syn-lacunar-pure-motor", "syn-brown-sequard", "syn-wallenberg-lateral-medullary", "syn-mca-superior-division", "syn-cerebellar-hemispheric"], topics=["topic-neuromuscular-patterns", "topic-neuroimaging-basics"],
 pitfalls=["Functional (non-organic) weakness is a diagnosis of positive signs (Hoover sign, give-way weakness, inconsistency), not of a normal scan"], tags=["localization", "examination", "approach"]))

E.append(topic("topic-neuroimaging-basics", "Neuroimaging basics: reading CT and MRI against the atlas", "imaging",
 "Computed tomography maps X-ray attenuation, so bone and acute blood are bright and edema, infarction and air are dark, and it answers the emergency questions of hemorrhage, mass effect, hydrocephalus and fracture in minutes; magnetic resonance imaging maps proton signal under different weightings, so it separates gray from white matter, shows edema and demyelination on T2 and FLAIR, restricted diffusion in acute infarction and abscess on DWI, blood products on gradient and susceptibility images, and barrier breakdown after gadolinium. Each sequence is a question, and the atlas's MRI slices, which use the T1 and T2 templates, are the normal against which every abnormal study is compared; the arterial and venous meshes give the vascular map for CT and MR angiography.",
 [("CT: density and the acute questions", "Hyperdensity means blood (acute hemorrhage, a thrombosed vessel, a hyperdense MCA sign), calcification, dense tumor or contrast; hypodensity means edema, infarction older than a few hours, old blood, fat or air. The early signs of ischemic stroke are subtle: loss of the insular ribbon, obscuration of the lentiform nucleus and effacement of sulci, graded by the ASPECTS score; a hemorrhage is obvious and its site (putamen, thalamus, pons, cerebellum for hypertension; lobar for amyloid angiopathy and malformations; basal cisterns for aneurysmal subarachnoid) names its cause. Mass effect shows as sulcal effacement, ventricular compression, midline shift and cisternal effacement in that order, and the temporal horns round early in hydrocephalus. CT angiography shows occlusions, aneurysms and dissections; CT perfusion separates the infarct core from the penumbra. CT is the test for trauma, for the first hours of stroke and for patients who cannot have MRI."),
  ("MRI: sequences as questions", "T1 shows anatomy with white matter bright and CSF dark, and fat, subacute blood, melanin, protein and gadolinium bright; T2 shows CSF and edema bright; FLAIR suppresses CSF so that periventricular and cortical lesions, subarachnoid blood and meningeal disease stand out; DWI with the ADC map shows restricted diffusion in acute infarction (bright DWI, dark ADC, for about ten days), abscess pus, dense tumors such as lymphoma and medulloblastoma, prion disease and status epilepticus; SWI and gradient echo show hemosiderin, microbleeds, cavernomas, calcification and venous structures; STIR suppresses fat for the spine and orbits; and gadolinium enhancement marks barrier breakdown in tumor, infection, inflammation, subacute infarction and demyelination. MRA and MRV image the arteries and veins without contrast. Contraindications are ferromagnetic implants and some devices; gadolinium is avoided in severe renal failure."),
  ("Patterns that localize the pathology", "An arterial infarct respects a territory and has a wedge shape; a venous infarct crosses territories, is often hemorrhagic and lies near a thrombosed sinus; a watershed infarct is linear along a border zone. Demyelination produces ovoid periventricular lesions perpendicular to the ventricles, juxtacortical, callosal, brainstem and cord lesions, some enhancing; small vessel disease produces patchy deep and periventricular hyperintensities with lacunes and microbleeds. Ring enhancement with central restricted diffusion is an abscess; ring enhancement without it is a necrotic tumor or, with a thin rim, a demyelinating lesion; multiple enhancing lesions at the gray-white junction are metastases; a dural-based enhancing mass with a tail is a meningioma; and leptomeningeal enhancement is infection, carcinomatosis or sarcoidosis. Symmetric deep gray matter signal change points to toxic, metabolic, hypoxic or prion disease; symmetric white-matter disease points to a leukodystrophy or toxic leukoencephalopathy."),
  ("The spine, the vessels and the pitfalls", "Spinal MRI uses sagittal T2 and STIR to find cord signal change, compression and the level, axial images to define foraminal and canal stenosis, and contrast for tumor, infection and inflammation; CT shows bone. Vascular imaging (CTA, MRA, DSA) is read against the atlas arteries: the site of an occlusion predicts the infarct, the branch point predicts the aneurysm, and the tapered stenosis with intramural hematoma is the dissection. Pitfalls include the normal bright posterior limb on FLAIR, ependymitis granularis at the frontal horns, the incompletely myelinated white matter of infants, the prominent flocculus and choroid plexus that mimic masses, flow artifacts in the sinuses that mimic thrombosis, and the age-related white-matter hyperintensities that are not multiple sclerosis; and every study must be read with the clinical localization in hand, because an incidental finding will otherwise become the diagnosis."),],
 ["CT for blood, bone and the first hour; MRI for everything else", "DWI is the sequence of the acute stroke and the abscess; FLAIR of demyelination and the periventricular lesion; SWI of blood and calcium; gadolinium of the open barrier", "Arterial infarcts respect territories; venous infarcts, tumors and infections do not", "Ring enhancement with restricted diffusion in the center is an abscess; with a thin, open ring it is demyelination", "Read the scan after the examination, not instead of it"],
 ["Compare every lesion with the atlas slice at the same coordinates: the normal is the best contrast agent", "A normal early DWI does not exclude a small brainstem or lacunar infarct; repeat it", "Symmetry on MRI means metabolic, toxic, hypoxic or genetic; asymmetry means vascular, neoplastic or inflammatory"],
 [B(2, 11, 24), B(19, 179, 194), B(21, 224, 228), S(17, 471, 475), S(1, 20, 32)], meshIds=["arteries-mra-atlas", "brain-envelope", "ventricle-lateral-l", "ventricle-lateral-r", "internal-capsule-l", "internal-capsule-r"],
 imaging=("The atlas MRI slices are the MNI T1 and T2 templates at 1 mm; the T1 shows white matter bright and the T2 shows CSF bright, and the label overlays name every structure at the cursor.",
  [pathol("Acute ischemic infarct", "MRI", "Bright on DWI with dark ADC within minutes, FLAIR-bright after 4-6 hours, T2-bright by 24 hours; enhancement in the subacute phase; encephalomalacia later.", sequence="DWI"),
   pathol("Intracerebral hemorrhage", "CT", "Hyperdense mass with surrounding hypodense edema; on MRI the signal evolves with the blood products (hyperacute T2-bright, acute T2-dark, subacute T1-bright, chronic hemosiderin rim).", sequence="CT non-contrast"),
   pathol("Brain abscess", "MRI", "Ring-enhancing lesion with central restricted diffusion and a smooth thin capsule that is T2-dark; the ring is thinner toward the ventricle.", sequence="DWI"),
   pathol("Multiple sclerosis", "MRI", "Ovoid periventricular, juxtacortical, infratentorial and spinal cord lesions on FLAIR and T2; some enhance; central veins on SWI.", sequence="FLAIR"),
   pathol("Glioblastoma", "MRI", "Irregular ring-enhancing mass with central necrosis, surrounding FLAIR infiltration and elevated perfusion; no central restricted diffusion.", sequence="T1+Gd")]),
 structures=["arteries-mra-atlas", "brain-envelope", "cerebral-white-matter", "insula", "ventricle-lateral"], syndromes=["syn-mca-stem-infarct", "syn-subarachnoid-hemorrhage", "syn-multiple-sclerosis-pattern", "syn-hydrocephalus"], topics=["topic-csf-physiology-blood-brain-barrier", "topic-stroke-mechanisms-hemorrhage", "topic-localization-approach-exam"],
 pitfalls=["The scan cannot tell you which lesion is symptomatic; the examination does", "Contrast enhancement in a subacute infarct mimics a tumor; the clinical time course and the vascular territory decide"], tags=["imaging", "CT", "MRI", "sequences"]))

E.append(topic("topic-stroke-mechanisms-hemorrhage", "Stroke mechanisms and intracranial hemorrhage", "disease-pattern",
 "A stroke is a sudden focal neurological deficit from a vascular cause, ischemic in about four-fifths and hemorrhagic in the rest, and its mechanism determines both the pattern of injury and the treatment: large artery atherosclerosis, cardioembolism, small vessel disease, dissection and rarer causes produce ischemic infarcts in the territories shown in this atlas, whereas hypertension, amyloid angiopathy, aneurysms, vascular malformations, venous thrombosis and anticoagulation produce hemorrhage into the parenchyma, the subarachnoid space or the subdural and epidural compartments. Localization gives the territory, the territory gives the artery, and the artery and the patient give the mechanism; recanalization within hours saves ischemic tissue, and the site and volume of a hemorrhage decide its danger.",
 [("Ischemic mechanisms and their imaging signatures", "Cardioembolism (atrial fibrillation, valve disease, recent infarction, endocarditis) sends clots to the large intracranial arteries and produces cortical infarcts in several territories or ages, often with hemorrhagic transformation; large artery atherosclerosis of the carotid bifurcation or the intracranial vessels produces territorial infarcts by artery-to-artery embolism and border-zone infarcts by hypoperfusion; small vessel lipohyalinosis of the lenticulostriate, thalamoperforating and pontine perforators produces lacunes with the classic lacunar syndromes; dissection of the carotid or vertebral artery in young patients produces neck pain, Horner syndrome and embolic infarcts; and rarer mechanisms include vasculitis, reversible vasoconstriction, hypercoagulable states, sickle cell disease, moyamoya and paradoxical embolism through a patent foramen ovale. The evaluation therefore includes vascular imaging from the aortic arch to the circle of Willis, cardiac rhythm monitoring and echocardiography, and the lipid and glucose profile that drives secondary prevention with antiplatelets or anticoagulants, statins and blood pressure control."),
  ("Acute ischemic stroke: time, core and penumbra", "Ischemic tissue dies at a rate that depends on collateral flow: the core is infarcted within minutes at the center of the territory, while the penumbra survives on leptomeningeal collaterals for hours and is the target of treatment. Intravenous thrombolysis within four and a half hours and mechanical thrombectomy for large vessel occlusion within six hours, or up to twenty-four hours when perfusion imaging shows a small core and a large penumbra, restore flow; the deficit, the NIH stroke scale, the CT ASPECTS score and the CTA occlusion site guide the decision, and hemorrhage, recent surgery and anticoagulation are the main contraindications. After reperfusion or without it, the infarct swells for three days, and malignant middle cerebral or cerebellar infarcts need decompressive surgery before herniation. Transient ischemic attacks carry a high early stroke risk and are investigated with the same urgency."),
  ("Intracerebral and subarachnoid hemorrhage", "Hypertensive hemorrhage arises from Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms of the perforating arteries and strikes the putamen, thalamus, pons and cerebellum; cerebral amyloid angiopathy of the elderly produces lobar hemorrhages at the cortical surface with microbleeds and superficial siderosis; arteriovenous malformations and cavernomas bleed in the young; anticoagulants, thrombolysis, tumors and venous thrombosis cause the rest. The hematoma expands in the first hours (the spot sign on CTA predicts it), and outcome depends on volume, location (brainstem and large deep hemorrhages are worst), ventricular extension and the level of consciousness; treatment is blood pressure lowering, reversal of anticoagulation and surgery for cerebellar hemorrhages over three centimeters or with brainstem compression. Aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage presents with a thunderclap headache, meningism and often a third nerve palsy from a posterior communicating aneurysm; it is complicated by rebleeding, vasospasm and delayed ischemia between days four and fourteen, hydrocephalus and hyponatremia, and is treated by coiling or clipping the aneurysm within a day."),
  ("Venous thrombosis and the extra-axial hemorrhages", "Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, from hypercoagulable states, pregnancy, infection and dehydration, raises pressure and produces headache, seizures and venous infarcts that cross arterial boundaries and often bleed; the superior sagittal sinus gives bilateral parasagittal lesions and the transverse sinus temporal lesions; MRV shows the absent flow and anticoagulation is the treatment despite the hemorrhage. Epidural hematoma from a middle meningeal artery tear under a temporal fracture produces a lucid interval and a lens-shaped clot that does not cross sutures; subdural hematoma from torn bridging veins produces a crescent that crosses sutures, acute in trauma and chronic in the elderly and anticoagulated, with fluctuating confusion and headache; both are surgical when they compress the brain. The atlas's venous meshes and the dural layers show why each collection has its shape.")],
 ["The territory names the artery; the patient names the mechanism; the mechanism chooses the prevention", "Cortical infarcts in more than one territory are embolic until proved otherwise; deep small infarcts are small vessel disease", "The penumbra is a collateral phenomenon: good collaterals buy time for thrombectomy", "Hypertension bleeds deep (putamen, thalamus, pons, cerebellum); amyloid bleeds lobar; aneurysms bleed into the cisterns", "Venous infarcts cross arterial territories and bleed, and their treatment is anticoagulation"],
 ["Time is brain: every minute of a large vessel occlusion costs nearly two million neurons", "A thunderclap headache with a third nerve palsy is a posterior communicating aneurysm until the CTA is done", "A cerebellar hemorrhage over three centimeters goes to the operating theater, not the ward"],
 [B(19, 179, 206), B(7, 53, 66), S(17, 463, 485), S(15, 422, 430)], meshIds=["arteries-mra-atlas", "artery-mca-l", "artery-mca-r", "artery-basilar", "putamen-l", "putamen-r", "sinus-superior-sagittal", "sinus-transverse-l", "sinus-transverse-r"],
 imaging=("Non-contrast CT first (hemorrhage, early ischemic signs, ASPECTS), then CTA of the arch-to-vertex vessels and CT perfusion or MRI with DWI for the core and penumbra; MRV for venous thrombosis; SWI for microbleeds and amyloid angiopathy; DSA for aneurysms and malformations.",
  [pathol("Large vessel occlusion with penumbra", "CT perfusion", "Small core (low cerebral blood volume) with a larger area of prolonged time-to-peak; the mismatch is the tissue thrombectomy can save.", sequence="n/a"),
   pathol("Hypertensive putaminal hemorrhage", "CT", "Hyperdense hematoma in the putamen extending into the capsule and ventricle; a spot sign on CTA predicts expansion.", sequence="CT non-contrast", timing="Repeat CT at 6 hours for expansion"),
   pathol("Cerebral amyloid angiopathy", "MRI", "Lobar hemorrhage with multiple cortical microbleeds and cortical superficial siderosis on SWI in an elderly patient.", sequence="SWI"),
   pathol("Aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage with vasospasm", "CTA", "Cisternal blood on CT, an aneurysm on CTA, and on days 4-14 narrowing of the proximal arteries with new perfusion deficits.", sequence="n/a"),
   pathol("Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis", "MRV", "Absent flow in the sagittal or transverse sinus with parasagittal or temporal hemorrhagic venous infarcts.", sequence="n/a")]),
 structures=["artery-mca", "artery-internal-carotid", "artery-basilar", "arteries-lenticulostriate", "circle-of-willis", "sinus-superior-sagittal", "veins-superficial-cerebral", "dura-mater"], syndromes=["syn-mca-stem-infarct", "syn-lacunar-pure-motor", "syn-basilar-occlusion", "syn-subarachnoid-hemorrhage", "syn-cerebral-venous-sinus-thrombosis", "syn-epidural-hematoma", "syn-subdural-hematoma", "syn-pontine-hemorrhage", "syn-watershed-infarct"], topics=["topic-neuroimaging-basics", "topic-headache-localization"],
 pitfalls=["A stroke in a young patient with neck pain is a dissection; do not attribute it to migraine", "Lobar hemorrhage in the elderly needs SWI for microbleeds before anticoagulation is ever considered again"], tags=["stroke", "hemorrhage", "vascular"]))

E.append(topic("topic-cns-infections-localization", "CNS infections by compartment", "infection",
 "Infections of the nervous system are localized by the compartment they occupy: the subarachnoid space and meninges (meningitis), the brain parenchyma (encephalitis and abscess), the epidural and subdural spaces (empyema and spinal epidural abscess), the ventricles (ventriculitis), the cord (myelitis) and the roots and nerves (radiculitis, neuritis). Each compartment has a clinical signature (meningism, altered consciousness with seizures and focal signs, a mass syndrome, back pain with a cord level), a route of entry (blood, contiguous sinus or ear, direct inoculation, retrograde along nerves) and an imaging and CSF pattern, and the pathogen is predicted by age, immune status, exposure and the compartment. Time matters most in bacterial meningitis and herpes encephalitis, where treatment precedes the confirmation.",
 [("Meningitis: the subarachnoid compartment", "Bacterial meningitis (pneumococcus, meningococcus, Listeria in the elderly and immunosuppressed, group B streptococcus and E. coli in neonates) produces fever, headache, neck stiffness, photophobia and declining consciousness within hours, with a purulent CSF (neutrophils, low glucose, high protein) and complications from the inflamed basal cisterns: cranial nerve palsies, hydrocephalus, vasculitic infarcts and cerebral edema; antibiotics and dexamethasone are given before the lumbar puncture when the puncture will be delayed, and a CT precedes the puncture when there is a focal deficit, papilledema, seizures or coma. Viral meningitis (enteroviruses, herpes simplex type 2, mumps) is self-limited with a lymphocytic CSF and normal glucose. Tuberculous and fungal (cryptococcal) meningitis are subacute basal meningitides with lymphocytes, low glucose and high protein, cranial nerve palsies, hydrocephalus and infarcts, and neoplastic meningitis mimics them. Rash and shock point to meningococcus; a recent sinus or ear infection points to pneumococcus and to a contiguous abscess."),
  ("Encephalitis and abscess: the parenchymal compartment", "Herpes simplex encephalitis attacks the medial temporal lobes, the insula and the orbitofrontal cortex, usually asymmetrically, producing fever, confusion, aphasia, seizures and memory loss, with a lymphocytic CSF, a positive PCR, temporal FLAIR change and periodic lateralized discharges on EEG; acyclovir is started at suspicion. Arboviral and enteroviral encephalitides involve the thalamus, basal ganglia and brainstem (Japanese and West Nile), rabies ascends the nerves to the brainstem and limbic system, and autoimmune encephalitis mimics all of these. A brain abscess follows contiguous spread from the sinuses (frontal lobe), the ear (temporal lobe and cerebellum) or hematogenous seeding (gray-white junction, multiple), producing a mass syndrome with headache, focal signs and seizures, often without fever; it enhances in a ring with central restricted diffusion and needs drainage and antibiotics. Toxoplasmosis (multiple ring lesions in the basal ganglia), progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (non-enhancing white-matter lesions) and cryptococcoma are the lesions of the immunosuppressed, and neurocysticercosis is the commonest cause of seizures worldwide."),
  ("Empyema, spinal infections and the roots", "Subdural empyema, usually from frontal sinusitis, is a surgical emergency with headache, fever, seizures and rapid deterioration and a thin enhancing collection over the convexity or along the falx; epidural abscess is slower. Spinal epidural abscess (staphylococcus from bacteremia, injection or surgery) produces back pain, fever, a radicular then a cord syndrome, and an enhancing epidural collection on MRI that compresses the cord and must be decompressed before paralysis; vertebral osteomyelitis and discitis accompany it. Infectious myelitis (enteroviruses, herpes viruses, HIV, syphilis, tuberculosis) produces a cord syndrome with CSF pleocytosis; poliomyelitis and its modern mimics attack the anterior horn with flaccid asymmetric paralysis. Radiculitis and neuritis follow herpes zoster (dermatomal pain and vesicles, with facial palsy in Ramsay Hunt), Lyme disease (facial palsy, painful radiculitis, meningitis), leprosy (thickened nerves with anesthetic patches), HIV and cytomegalovirus."),
  ("Chronic and treatable mimics", "Neurosyphilis (meningovascular stroke in the young, tabes dorsalis with lightning pains and Argyll Robertson pupils, general paresis with dementia) and tuberculosis are the great imitators and are treatable; HIV produces a meningitis at seroconversion, a subcortical dementia, a vacuolar myelopathy, neuropathies and the opportunistic infections; prion disease is a transmissible but not infectious mimic of rapid dementia; and Whipple disease, Lyme neuroborreliosis and fungal infections present as chronic meningitis or encephalopathy. Every chronic meningitis therefore has a CSF sent for mycobacterial culture, cryptococcal antigen, syphilis serology and cytology, and every unexplained encephalopathy has an HIV test. The compartmental logic, meninges versus parenchyma versus epidural space versus roots, remains the fastest route to the right test and the right drug.")],
 ["Meningism means the subarachnoid space; confusion with seizures and focal signs means the parenchyma; back pain with a cord level means the epidural space", "Neutrophils and low glucose is bacterial; lymphocytes with low glucose is tuberculosis, fungus or cancer; lymphocytes with normal glucose is viral", "Herpes encephalitis lives in the medial temporal lobe and the insula and spares the putamen; start acyclovir before the PCR returns", "An abscess enhances in a ring with restricted diffusion in the center and is drained, not biopsied", "Spinal epidural abscess is back pain, fever and a rising deficit: MRI and surgery the same day"],
 ["Give the antibiotic before the scan when meningitis is likely and the scan will take an hour", "A ring lesion in an immunosuppressed patient is toxoplasmosis, lymphoma or abscess; the diffusion and the response to treatment separate them", "Facial palsy with a rash in the ear or a summer tick bite is not Bell palsy"],
 [B(20, 207, 222), B(2, 20, 24), S(15, 417, 435), S(16, 456, 462)], meshIds=["hippocampus-l", "hippocampus-r", "insula-l", "insula-r", "spinal-dura", "cn-07-facial-l", "cn-07-facial-r"],
 imaging=("Contrast MRI is the study for every compartment: leptomeningeal enhancement and cisternal exudate in meningitis, temporal and insular FLAIR change in herpes, ring enhancement with restricted diffusion in abscess, extra-axial enhancing collections in empyema, and epidural enhancing collections with cord compression in the spine; CT precedes lumbar puncture when mass effect is possible.",
  [pathol("Bacterial meningitis", "MRI", "Leptomeningeal enhancement over the convexity and in the basal cisterns with FLAIR-bright sulcal exudate; hydrocephalus and infarcts in complicated cases.", sequence="T1+Gd"),
   pathol("Herpes simplex encephalitis", "MRI", "Asymmetric medial temporal, insular and orbitofrontal FLAIR hyperintensity with cortical restricted diffusion and petechial hemorrhage; the putamen is spared.", sequence="FLAIR", timing="Changes are present within 48 hours; CT is often normal"),
   pathol("Brain abscess", "MRI", "Ring-enhancing lesion with a thin, smooth capsule and central restricted diffusion; satellite lesions and surrounding edema.", sequence="DWI"),
   pathol("Tuberculous meningitis", "MRI", "Thick basal cisternal enhancement, hydrocephalus, infarcts of the perforator territories and tuberculomas.", sequence="T1+Gd"),
   pathol("Spinal epidural abscess", "MRI", "Enhancing epidural collection with cord compression, adjacent discitis and vertebral marrow edema on STIR.", sequence="T1+Gd")]),
 structures=["subarachnoid-space-cisterns", "hippocampus", "insula", "temporal-pole", "spinal-dura", "meningeal-spaces", "dorsal-root-ganglion"], syndromes=["syn-hydrocephalus", "syn-ramsay-hunt", "syn-facial-palsy-peripheral-vs-central", "syn-poliomyelitis", "syn-transverse-myelitis", "syn-argyll-robertson"], topics=["topic-csf-physiology-blood-brain-barrier", "topic-neuroimaging-basics", "topic-brain-tumor-localization"],
 pitfalls=["A normal CSF glucose does not exclude bacterial meningitis early or after antibiotics; treat the picture", "Temporal lobe signal change with fever is herpes until the PCR says otherwise; autoimmune encephalitis is the next thought"], tags=["infection", "meningitis", "encephalitis", "abscess"]))

write(E)
